::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.6
Question:
A shirt comes in 4 colors and 3 sizes. How many shirt options are possible?
{
=A. 12#Correct. Multiply 4 colors by 3 sizes.
~B. 7#This adds choices instead of multiplying.
~C. 4#This counts only colors.
~D. 3#This counts only sizes.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.6
Question:
A lunch has 2 entrees, 3 sides, and 2 drinks. How many lunches are possible?
{
~A. 7#This adds choices instead of multiplying decision steps.
=B. 12#Correct. Multiply 2 times 3 times 2 \= 12.
~C. 10#This omits one decision step.
~D. 6#This counts only entrees and sides.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.7
Question:
Five students run for president and vice president. How many ordered outcomes are possible?
{
~A. 10#This is too small because each first choice has four second choices.
~B. 25#This allows the same person to hold both offices.
=C. 20#Correct. There are 5 choices for president and 4 remaining choices for vice president.
~D. 5#This counts only president choices.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.7
Question:
Five students are available and two are chosen for the same committee. How many groups are possible?
{
~A. 20#This counts ordered positions, but the committee roles are the same.
~B. 25#This allows repeated students and counts order.
~C. 5#This counts individual students, not pairs.
=D. 10#Correct. There are 20 ordered pairs, and each group is counted twice, so 10 groups.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.6
Question:
A code has 3 letters followed by 4 digits, with choices already limited to 3 letter symbols and 4 digit symbols for each position. How many one-letter-one-digit codes are possible?
{
=A. 12#Correct. One letter choice and one digit choice give 3 times 4 \= 12 codes.
~B. 7#This adds the choices instead of multiplying.
~C. 34#This writes the numbers side by side instead of counting outcomes.
~D. 1#This ignores the choices available.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.7
Question:
Which situation has order that matters?
{
~A. Choosing two equal members for a committee#Equal committee roles do not depend on order.
=B. Choosing a first-place and second-place winner#Correct. First place and second place are different positions.
~C. Selecting three toppings for a pizza#The selected toppings are usually the same no matter the order named.
~D. Picking two books to donate#The donation set is usually unchanged by order.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.7
Question:
Which situation is best described as a combination?
{
~A. Assigning president and treasurer#Different offices mean order or role matters.
~B. Ranking three finalists#Ranking depends on order.
=C. Choosing three people for the same team#Correct. The same team does not change when names are listed in a different order.
~D. Awarding gold, silver, and bronze#Awards are ordered positions.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.6
Question:
A restaurant offers 5 main dishes and 2 desserts. How many main-dish-and-dessert choices are possible?
{
~A. 7#This adds choices instead of multiplying.
~B. 5#This counts only main dishes.
~C. 2#This counts only desserts.
=D. 10#Correct. Multiply 5 by 2 to count paired choices.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.6
Question:
A lock code has 2 choices for the first position and 6 choices for the second position. How many codes are possible?
{
=A. 12#Correct. Multiply 2 by 6.
~B. 8#This adds choices instead of multiplying.
~C. 6#This counts only the second position.
~D. 4#This does not use both decision steps.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.7
Question:
Six students run for president and secretary. How many ordered results are possible?
{
~A. 12#This doubles the number of students but does not count ordered pairs.
=B. 30#Correct. There are 6 choices for president and 5 remaining choices for secretary.
~C. 15#This would count unordered pairs.
~D. 36#This allows the same student to fill both offices.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.7
Question:
Six students are available and two are chosen for the same project team. How many teams are possible?
{
~A. 30#This counts ordered roles, but the project team roles are the same.
~B. 12#This doubles the students and does not count pairs.
=C. 15#Correct. Six times five gives 30 ordered pairs, then divide by 2 to get 15 teams.
~D. 36#This allows repeated students and counts order.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.6
Question:
A survey asks one question with 4 answer choices and another with 3 answer choices. How many answer patterns are possible?
{
~A. 7#This adds answer choices instead of multiplying.
~B. 4#This counts only the first question.
~C. 3#This counts only the second question.
=D. 12#Correct. Each first answer can pair with each second answer, so 4 times 3 \= 12.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.7
Question:
What is the main question to ask before deciding between permutation and combination?
{
=A. Does order matter?#Correct. Order matters for permutations and does not matter for combinations.
~B. Is the answer always even?#The parity of the answer does not determine the method.
~C. Are there exactly two choices?#Both methods can involve more than two choices.
~D. Is there a table?#Tables are not required for counting methods.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.6
Question:
A snack pack has 3 cracker choices, 2 fruit choices, and 4 drink choices. How many packs are possible?
{
~A. 9#This adds the choices.
~B. 6#This counts only crackers and fruit.
~C. 12#This omits one decision step.
=D. 24#Correct. Multiply 3 times 2 times 4 \= 24.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.7
Question:
Four finalists are ranked first, second, and third. How many ordered rankings are possible?
{
~A. 4#This counts only first-place choices.
~B. 12#This counts first and second only.
=C. 24#Correct. There are 4 choices, then 3, then 2: 4 times 3 times 2 \= 24.
~D. 64#This allows repeats and does not match ranking without repeats.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.7
Question:
Four people are available and three are chosen for the same group. How many groups are possible?
{
~A. 24#This counts ordered rankings, not same-role groups.
~B. 12#This still overcounts the same groups.
~C. 3#This counts the selected spots, not the groups.
=D. 4#Correct. Choosing 3 from 4 leaves out one person each time, so there are 4 groups.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.6
Question:
A badge has 5 color choices and 5 icon choices. How many badges are possible?
{
=A. 25#Correct. Multiply 5 colors by 5 icons.
~B. 10#This adds choices instead of multiplying.
~C. 5#This counts only one decision step.
~D. 55#This writes the numbers side by side.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.7
Question:
Seven runners finish first and second. How many ordered top-two results are possible?
{
~A. 14#This doubles the runners but does not count ordered outcomes.
=B. 42#Correct. First place has 7 choices and second place has 6 remaining choices.
~C. 21#This counts unordered pairs.
~D. 49#This allows the same runner to finish both first and second.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.7
Question:
Seven students are available and two are chosen for the same committee. How many committees are possible?
{
~A. 42#This counts order, but committee order does not matter.
~B. 14#This doubles the number of students.
=C. 21#Correct. Seven times six gives 42 ordered pairs, and dividing by 2 gives 21 committees.
~D. 49#This allows repeated students.
}


::MCR_U05_L05_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L05_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.6
Question:
An outfit has 2 hat choices, 4 shirt choices, and 3 shoe choices. How many outfits are possible?
{
~A. 9#This adds choices instead of multiplying.
~B. 12#This omits the hats or another decision step.
~C. 18#This does not multiply all three choices correctly.
=D. 24#Correct. Multiply 2 times 4 times 3 \= 24.
}

